WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:05.600
Despite its seriousness, Avengers: Infinity
War is at times a pretty lighthearted movie,

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.310
and in keeping with Marvel Studios tradition,
a lot of its best moments are totally improvised.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:14.460
We're getting into major spoilers for Infinity
War, so turn back now if you're one of the

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.199
three people on Earth who hasn't seen it.

00:00:16.199 --> 00:00:20.530
A new interview with the film's screenwriters
on Yahoo Entertainment has shed light on one

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:24.890
of Infinity War's biggest laugh-out-loud moments,
revealing it to be a clever bit of improv

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.500
from Dave Bautista, who plays Drax.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.700
According to Christopher Markus, who co-wrote
the script for Infinity War with Stephen McFeely,

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:36.320
Bautista made a significant addition the script,
punching the scene up by throwing in an extra

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.730
quip for Drax at the end of one exchange.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:39.730
Markus explained:

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:42.100
"The script only said, 'Where is Gamora?'

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.060
'I'll do you even better: Who is Gamora?'

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:48.239
And then one day Bautista just goes, 'I'll
do you one better: Why is Gamora?'

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:50.829
It's like, 'OK, you're very good at your job.'"

00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:55.190
The hilarious Drax line is just one of the
examples from Infinity War of an actor elevating

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:58.140
what was in the script to something even more
resonant.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.670
According to the movie's co-director Joe Russo,
Spider-Man actor Tom Holland completely improvised

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:07.200
his final scene, after Thanos successfully
managed to disintegrate half of all life in

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.200
the universe.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.600
Thanks to the actor's heartbreakingly realistic
performance, his disintegration hit many viewers

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.420
the hardest of them all, while also inspiring
a pretty solid meme.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.959
The improvisation in Infinity War is par for
the course for Marvel Studios, which has reportedly

00:01:20.959 --> 00:01:24.369
produced entire films that are composed mostly
of improv.

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:29.049
Jeff Bridges revealed years ago that much
of 2008's Iron Man was essentially improvised,

00:01:29.049 --> 00:01:30.049
saying:

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:31.049
"They had an outline.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:34.369
We would show up for big scenes every day
and we wouldn't know what we were going to

00:01:34.369 --> 00:01:35.369
say."

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:39.719
"All the guys are in the studio, tapping their
foot, looking at the watch, and we're in my

00:01:39.719 --> 00:01:43.579
trailer trying to figure out our lines, man!"

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:47.670
The loose approach for that first film wasn't
necessarily an accident, as Don Cheadle has

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.950
said that Iron Man 2 also lacked a locked-in
script, explaining:

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.999
"To be honest, we did improv a lot.

00:01:52.999 --> 00:01:56.829
We were very often sort of on a journey of
discovery in the scenes."

00:01:56.829 --> 00:02:01.040
While other movies in the MCU have generally
had completed scripts, most of them have at

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.260
times allowed an atmosphere of improvisation.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:09.100
"When you're standing around set for hours,
you think of dumb stuff to say, and sometimes

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:09.720
you say it when they're rolling."

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:14.390
One of those moments took place in Captain
America: Civil War, during Tony Stark's first

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.150
scene with Peter Parker.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:19.480
According to the movie's official commentary
track, Holland forgot the blocking of the

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.780
scene while they were shooting it, and Downey
telling him to "move the leg" was his way

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.280
of saving the scene while staying in character.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:30.380
"I'm gonna sit here, so you move the leg."

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:36.860
The second movie in the MCU, The Incredible
Hulk, also featured an entire scene of improv.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.830
William Hurt said that his post-credit scene
as General Ross didn't have any lines written

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:41.820
down.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.440
"I hear you have an unusual problem."

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.220
"You should talk."

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.230
"You should listen."

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.860
Each Avengers movie has at least one great
little improvised moment.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:56.040
Tony Stark offered Captain America some blueberries
in The Avengers because Downey had a habit

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:59.060
of hiding snacks around set and taking them
out during scenes.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:03.180
"In a few hours, I'll know every dirty secret
S.H.I.E.L.D. has ever tried to hide. Blueberry?"

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.260
In Age of Ultron, Jeremy Renner took a moment
to ad-lib a joke about shooting Quicksilver

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:08.830
in the midst of all the action.

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:10.450
"Nobody would know.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:11.440
Nobody.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.400
'And the last I saw him was when Ultron was
sitting on him', yeah I'll be missed.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.870
Quick little bastard."

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:23.250
But the most improv-heavy movie in the MCU
is likely Thor: Ragnarok, according to director

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:24.370
Taika Waititi.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:28.260
"I would say we improvised probably 80 percent
of the film."

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.050
It's an approach that feels risky, but with
enough talent in the room, it's apparently

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:33.050
an approach that works.

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.760
"Time works real different around these parts.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.560
On any other world I'd be like millions of
years old, but here on Sakar…"

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.260
Thanks for watching!

